   "source": [
    "# Section 2: Fitting vapour pressure\n",
    "\n",
    "There is no simple relationship between flash point and basic molecular features, although advanced machine learning models have been able to predict flash points with reasonable accuracy (e.g. [Sun et al., 2020](https://doi.org/10.1002/minf.201900101)).\n",
    "\n",
    "There is, however, a definite link between the vapour pressure of a liquid and its flash point - the flash point requires sufficient vapour pressure above the liquid to form an ignitable mixture with air. There must also therefore be a link with boiling point; recall that boiling occurs when the vapour pressure matches the external pressure.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, you will investigate how the vapour pressure of a liquid varies with temperature, and consider how this can relate to the flash point of a liquid.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Vapour pressure model\n",
    "\n",
    "The vapour pressure of a liquid can be determined by the [Clausius-Clapeyron equation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clausius%E2%80%93Clapeyron_relation), but this can be difficult to apply in practice. A more practical approach is to use the [Antoine equation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine_equation), which is a semi-empirical relationship between vapour pressure and temperature:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\log_{10} \\mathrm{P} = A - \\frac{B}{C + \\mathrm{T}}\\ ,$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\mathrm{P}$ is the vapour pressure in bar, $\\mathrm{T}$ is the temperature in Kelvin, and $A$, $B$, and $C$ are compound-specific constants. By measuring the vapour pressure of a liquid at different temperatures and fitting the Antoine equation to the data, these constants can be determined for each compound.\n"
   ]
  },
