    "# Section 3: Molecular volumes\n",
    "\n",
    "Ideally, we would like to predict the vapour pressure of a liquid based on its molecular properties, in order to predict the flash point. Although there are many factors that influence vapour pressure, one of the most significant is the molecular surface area (molecules with larger surface areas have higher vapour pressures).\n",
    "Calculating molecular surface area is not straightforward, therefore we will approximate it.\n",
    "\n",
    "In general, larger molecules will have larger surface areas. We can imagine wrapping an ellipsoid tightly around the molecule, and measuring its resulting surface area:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"./trig_ellipsoid_highres.png\" alt=\"Ellipsoid wrapped around an octahedral molecule\" width=\"50%\"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "Principal component analysis (PCA) is a method to find the directions in which a dataset varies the most. In this case, we can use PCA to find the three orthogonal directions in which a molecule is largest, shown as yellow arrows above. These directions define the principal axes of the ellipsoid which fits around the molecule, and can be used to calculate its surface area.\n",
    "\n",
    "PCA is a good example of linear algebra in action, but see [here](https://setosa.io/ev/principal-component-analysis/) for a more visual explanation. To find the principal axes and then the surface area from molecular coordinates, the steps are:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Start with the molecule centred at (0,0,0)\n",
    "    > NOTE: The molecular coordinates provided in task 3.1 have already been centred for you.\n",
    "2. Calculate the $3\\times3$ covariance matrix $M$ between $x$,$y$ and $z$ coordinates for all atoms:\n",
    "\n",
    "   $$ M = \\begin{pmatrix}\n",
    "   \\mathrm{cov}(x,x) & \\mathrm{cov}(x,y) & \\mathrm{cov}(x,z) \\\\\n",
    "   \\mathrm{cov}(y,x) & \\mathrm{cov}(y,y) & \\mathrm{cov}(y,z) \\\\\n",
    "   \\mathrm{cov}(z,x) & \\mathrm{cov}(z,y) & \\mathrm{cov}(z,z)\n",
    "   \\end{pmatrix}, $$\n",
    "\n",
    "   where, for example, $\\mathrm{cov}(x,y)$  is the covariance between the $x$ and $y$ coordinates representing how much one varies with the other. For a set of $n$ coordinates, the covariance between, for example, $x$ and $y$ is given by\n",
    "\n",
    "   $$ \\mathrm{cov}(x,y) = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i}y_{i}}{n}, $$\n",
    "\n",
    "    which is closely related to the dot product between the vectors of $x$ and $y$ coordinates.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Calculate the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $M$. The eigenvectors give the directions the ellipsoid axes point, and the **square root** of the eigenvalues give their lengths ($a$,$b$ and $c$).\n",
    "    > Hint: Eigenvectors and eigenvalues are easily calculated using the Numpy function `numpy.linalg.eigh`\n",
    "4. Taking the square root of the eigenvalues as the ellipsoid principal axes $a,b,c$, calculate the ellipsoid surface area $S$ using the approximate formula\n",
