    "#It took me a while to work out how to the covariance matrix \n",
    "#luckily help with PCA is readily available\n",
    "#first I defined a function that computes the surface area.\n",
    "def compute_surface_area(coords_file):\n",
    "    df = pd.read_csv(coords_file)\n",
    "    coords = df[[\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]].values  # already centered\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Covariance matrix (3×3) = M\n",
    "    M = np.cov(coords, rowvar=False, bias=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Here I define Eigenvalues / eigenvectors\n",
    "    eigenvals, eigenvecs = np.linalg.eigh(M)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Ellipsoid semi-axes\n",
    "    a, b, c = np.sqrt(eigenvals)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Then approximate surface area with the above formula\n",
    "    S = 4 * np.pi * ((a*b)**1.6 + (a*c)**1.6 + (b*c)**1.6) / 3**0.6\n",
    "\n",
    "    return S, (a, b, c)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Molecule renamed, molecule used before \n",
    "coord_files = {\n",
    "    \"Ethane\":        \"ethane_coordinates.csv\",\n",
    "    \"Benzene\":       \"benzene_coordinates.csv\",\n",
    "    \"Bromoethane\":   \"bromoethane_coordinates.csv\",\n",
    "    \"Furfural\":      \"furfural_coordinates.csv\"\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "surface_areas = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n\\n================ Molecular Surface Areas ================\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for mol, file in coord_files.items():\n",
    "    S, axes = compute_surface_area(file)\n",
    "    surface_areas[mol] = S\n",
    "    print(f\"{mol:12s}  Surface area = {S:8.3f} Å²   Axes = {axes}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to DataFrame\n",
    "sa_df = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(surface_areas, orient=\"index\", columns=[\"SurfaceArea\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "#Discussion \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "DISCUSSION\n",
    "\n",
    "  PCA gave us an approximate 3D size of each molecule based on the\n",
    "  covariance of its atomic coordinates.\n",
    "\n",
    "From our results so far\n",
    "  Vapour pressure:\n",
    "    – Larger surface areas tend to give lower vapour pressures.\n",
    "      This suggests lower volatility\n",
    "\n",
    "  When we compare surface area with flash point:\n",
    "    – The molecules with larger surface areas had higher\n",
    "      flash points, again consistent with lower volatility.\n",
    "\n",
    "This makes sense as these larger molecules will encur larger London forces, lower volatility\n",
    "\n",
    "  correlations are not perfect because:\n",
    "    – PCA ellipsoid approximation ignores atomic radii.\n",
    "    – Molecular interactions (H-bonding, polarity, halogens) have\n",
    "      very large effects not captured by surface area alone.\n",
    "    – Molecules may adopt different conformations in reality.\n",
    "\n",
    "  Improvements:\n",
    "    – Use van der Waals surface area (e.g., RDKit molecular SASA).\n",
    "    – Include atomic radii in the PCA model.\n",
    "    – Use multiple conformers rather than a single geometry.\n",
    "    – Include additional descriptors: molar mass, polarity, dipole\n",
    "      moment, number of H-bond donors/acceptors.\n",
    "      \n",
    "I think PCA is a good way to find molecular size and is useful as an indicator/explanation for flashpoint \n",
    "and vapour pressure values \n",
    "\"\"\")\n",
